# The LFS filters for bin/*.rpm and bin/*.exe were removed on 2026-08-22,
# when the Dell artefacts stopped being tracked at all — see bin/README.md
# and .gitignore. Nothing under bin/ is in the index now, so a filter there
# has nothing to act on.
#
# That also retires the `bin/emc_cee_RHEL-9.2.0.0.x86_64.rpm !filter !diff
# !merge` exemption added in 1ce79f0. It was the right fix for the problem
# it addressed — the glob matched a file that predates LFS, so git ran the
# clean filter on a 4 MB blob and reported it as a 132-byte pointer, a
# phantom diff that survived `git restore` — but an exemption from a filter
# that no longer exists is dead weight. Untracking the artefacts dissolves
# the same phantom diff at the source.
#
# They are still LFS pointers in *history*, which is why they check out
# correctly at older commits: git reads .gitattributes from the commit being
# checked out, not from HEAD. The RHEL rpm is the one exception — an
# ordinary 4 MB blob throughout. None of that changes by removing the lines
# here; only a history rewrite would.
#
# Do not re-add these filters. Their original comment warned that the
# pattern matched unconditionally, so any `git add` of a vendor artefact
# converted it to a pointer silently; .gitignore now stops the add itself,
# which is the better guard.

# Vendor PDFs under docs/ are small enough to keep as ordinary blobs, but
# git's binary heuristic only inspects the first 8000 bytes and misses at
# least one of them — docs/cee-9-x-windows-guide_en-us.pdf diffs as ~2000
# lines of mojibake. Mark them explicitly so `git diff` and `git log -p`
# say "Binary files differ" instead.
*.pdf binary
